= The Exiles' Question

It begins with a banker, then a shipping family, then a retired general who had been careful for twelve years — one after another, men of standing stop being coy about the Empire. They attend the legation's receptions under their own names. They subscribe, publicly, to funds for the relief of the marches. One of them tells a newspaper that he sees no contradiction between loving the Commonwealth and believing it belongs somewhere.

The <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> are losing people over it, and losing them in a direction their leadership did not expect. Their monarchism was always a domestic proposition: crown, land, faith, and a firm hand at home. The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> offer something simpler and, to a certain kind of man, far more attractive — an actual living Empress, an actual functioning empire, and an end to the argument.

The uncomfortable conclusion of the Interior's own report is that we have been counting Restorationists wrong for years. We counted the ones who march. We did not count the ones who merely wait, and there are considerably more of the second kind.

- @troubling
- @forget_past

@troubling
title: Troubling news, to be sure.
subtitle: Treat it as what it is - a security problem.
on-arrival: empire_posture -= 4 if empire_posture >= 4

We treat the report as a warning rather than a curiosity. The names are noted, the funds are watched, and the legation is left in no doubt that its receptions are counted. It is the correct response to a foreign power cultivating a domestic clientele, and the legation understands it precisely that way.

@forget_past
title: It is hard to forget the past.
subtitle: These men are not traitors. They are old, and the Empire is what they remember.
on-arrival: empire_posture += 4 if empire_posture <= 96

We decline to make enemies of them. Most were grown men when the Commonwealth was declared; they buried sons in Imperial uniforms and were told afterward that the country those sons died for had been a mistake. That is not treason. It is grief with a return address.

We say so publicly, and the legation notes — accurately — that the Commonwealth's largest party has just described affection for the Empire as something other than a crime.
